1. The array method `sort()` without parameters sorts elements as strings by default. This leads to incorrect ordering for numbers. For example "19" sorts before "5" because it compares character codes  
2. After sorting ascending order the second largest number is at the second last index not at index 1  
3. Sorting the entire array has O(n log n) complexity but we only need the second largest number which can be achieved more efficiently with O(n) by a single pass through the array  

Below is the corrected and optimized function that addresses these issues  

```javascript
function secondLargest(arr) {
    // Edge case: If array length is less than 2 return null because second largest does not exist
    if (arr.length < 2) return null;

    // Initialize two variables to hold the largest and second largest values
    // Set largest to the smaller possible number to start comparison
    // Use -Infinity to support negative numbers correctly
    let largest = -Infinity;
    let secondLargest = -Infinity;

    // Traverse each element in the array once
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        let num = arr[i];

        // Skip if current number is equal to largest or secondLargest to handle duplicates correctly
        // If current number is greater than largest then update largest and secondLargest accordingly
        if (num > largest) {
            secondLargest = largest;
            largest = num;
        } 
        // If current number is less than largest but greater than secondLargest then update secondLargest only
        else if (num > secondLargest && num < largest) {
            secondLargest = num;
        }
